## 3. Taṇhāmūlaka Sutta

23. "Bhikkhus, I will teach nine things rooted in craving. Listen to that. And what, bhikkhus, are the nine things rooted in craving?

Dependent on craving, there is search;
dependent on search, there is gain;
dependent on gain, there is decision;
dependent on decision, there is desire and lust;
dependent on desire and lust, there is attachment;
dependent on attachment, there is possession;
dependent on possession, there is stinginess;
dependent on stinginess, there is safeguarding;
because of safeguarding, there arise various evil unwholesome states: the taking up of sticks and weapons, quarrels, disputes, strife, insults, slander, and falsehood. These, bhikkhus, are the nine things rooted in craving."

The Third Sutta.

## 4. Sattāvāsa Sutta

24. "Bhikkhus, there are these nine abodes of beings. What nine?

Bhikkhus, there are beings diverse in body and diverse in perception, such as human beings, some devas, and some in the fallen realms. This is the first abode of beings.

Bhikkhus, there are beings diverse in body but uniform in perception, such as the devas of the Brahma-group who are first reborn. This is the second abode of beings.

Bhikkhus, there are beings uniform in body but diverse in perception, such as the Ābhassara devas. This is the third abode of beings.

Bhikkhus, there are beings uniform in body and uniform in perception, such as the Subhakiṇha devas. This is the fourth abode of beings.

Bhikkhus, there are beings without perception and without feeling, such as the non-percipient devas. This is the fifth abode of beings."
